# PART V — THE SIGNATURE CAPABILITY

## AGENT-TO-AGENT DEFENSE

This is the most important differentiating capability.

The platform must understand interactions among autonomous agents.

Consider this scenario:

An organization's internal AI agent has access to approved repositories and development tools.

Untrusted repository content contains a malicious instruction.

The agent attempts an unexpected tool operation.

HF-CDF must:

1. Identify the registered agent.
2. Validate its identity.
3. Retrieve its approved task scope.
4. Retrieve its permitted tool set.
5. Observe the tool invocation.
6. Evaluate the action against deterministic policy.
7. Identify any scope violation.
8. Block the operation through the enforcement gateway before execution.
9. Create an evidence-backed security event.
10. Have Blue Force investigate the incident.
11. Correlate related identities, resources and activity.
12. Create a response recommendation.
13. Obtain human approval where needed.
14. Record the action and outcome.
15. Run an authorized isolated Red Force validation scenario.
16. Verify that the defensive control works.

Build this workflow with real persistent data and automated tests.

Do not represent it using a fabricated animation.

